# Cloudflare `_headers` — security headers applied by the Worker runtime.
#
# Must live at the PROJECT ROOT, not in static/ — adapter-cloudflare throws on a static/_headers.
# The adapter copies this file to the build output and appends its autogenerated immutable-asset
# caching block, so keep only hand-owned rules here.
#
# These close three findings the `josh-app dast` ZAP baseline scan reports on a bare project:
# missing anti-clickjacking [10020], X-Content-Type-Options [10021], and Permissions-Policy
# [10063]. Removing a line re-opens the corresponding finding and fails the scan.
#
# Not set here: Content-Security-Policy — and that is settled, not an open item. `svelte.config.js`
# sets `kit.csp`, so SvelteKit emits the policy itself on every page it renders: a real header with
# a per-request nonce on SSR pages, a <meta> tag with script hashes on prerendered ones. That is
# what closes ZAP's CSP Header Not Set [10038], and it is a shape a static file cannot reproduce —
# a nonce has to change per request, and a pasted hash goes stale on the next build.
#
# So never add a CSP line here. `_headers` decorates static-asset responses, which Worker-rendered
# SSR pages bypass entirely, leaving the line dead exactly where it reads most reassuring. On
# prerendered pages it does apply, and a browser enforces every delivered policy at once — a
# hash-less `script-src` would intersect with SvelteKit's and block the hydration script the hashes
# exist to allow. Adjust the directives in `kit.csp` instead (app-kit's `svelte.config.js` carries
# the block to copy; `josh-app sync` does not distribute it). A project that genuinely needs a
# header-based CSP on SSR passes it to `apply_security_headers`'s `extra` in `hooks.server.ts`.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()
